88,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,92,19788,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,198,59,2533,1654,1184,311,8253,892,3793,304,419,14461,525,24438,13,362,4647,374,24438,421,2176,1124,11520,26888,90,17,11035,8,323,1124,11520,26888,58,18,15370,18,11035,8,525,9226,311,7546,13430,429,1102,304,24438,5109,13,44763,11,1124,1188,59,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,11035,8,374,24438,421,17767,16,17,12646,57758,374,1496,11,323,1124,1188,59,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,57758,374,24438,421,17767,74,57758,374,264,5248,315,220,18,13,15277,11,17767,74,57758,1969,26553,2176,4682,25,17767,74,57758,1969,387,264,5248,315,220,18,323,17767,16,17,12646,57758,1969,387,1496,382,10061,594,1140,279,3204,2750,315,17767,74,59,1648,1406,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4a4dc36e068012b42b44355da66bbc107a4af9fb1137c9227ef4d52ca240741f"},"seconds":25.472561597824097,"seed":346765203911833118,"stop":"eos","text":"Let's list the possible values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n\n1. \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3.\n2. \\(12-k\\) must be even, which is always true if \\(k\\) is even since 12 is even.\n\nThe multiples of 3 up to 12 are 0, 3, 6, 9, and 12. We check which of these are even: 0, 6, and 12.\n\nFor these values of \\(k\\), we will calculate the corresponding terms and sum them up.\n\n- When \\(k=0\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{12} \\cdot 1 = 2^6 = 64\n  \\]\n\n- When \\(k=6\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = 924 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n  \\]\n\n- When \\(k=12\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n  \\]\n\nSo, the sum of all rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Let's list the possible values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n\n1. \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3.\n2. \\(12-k\\) must be even, which is always true if \\(k\\) is even since 12 is even.\n\nThe multiples of 3 up to 12 are 0, 3, 6, 9, and 12. We check which of these are even: 0, 6, and 12.\n\nFor these values of \\(k\\), we will calculate the corresponding terms and sum them up.\n\n- When \\(k=0\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{12} \\cdot 1 = 2^6 = 64\n  \\]\n\n- When \\(k=6\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = 924 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n  \\]\n\n- When \\(k=12\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n  \\]\n\nSo, the sum of all rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:445"}
